# OneKey Hardware SDK Example Project - Coding Rules

## Project Overview
This is a React + TypeScript web application for testing and demonstrating OneKey hardware wallet SDK functionality. The project uses modern web technologies with a focus on clean, maintainable code and consistent user experience.

## Tech Stack & Dependencies
- **Framework:** React 18+ with TypeScript
- **Routing:** React Router DOM
- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS with custom design system
- **UI Components:** Radix UI primitives with custom wrappers
- **Icons:** Lucide React
- **Internationalization:** i18next with react-i18next
- **Hardware SDK:** @onekeyfe/hd-core for hardware wallet integration
- **State Management:** Custom hooks and context providers
- **Build Tool:** Vite
- **Component Variants:** Class Variance Authority (CVA)

## Code Style & Patterns

### React Components
- Use functional components with TypeScript interfaces
- Prefer named exports over default exports for components
- Use React hooks for state management and side effects
- Follow the pattern: `const ComponentName: React.FC<Props> = ({ ...props }) => { ... }`
- Always define TypeScript interfaces for component props
- Use descriptive prop names and include JSDoc comments for complex props

### TypeScript Guidelines
- Use strict TypeScript configuration
- Define interfaces for all data structures
- Use union types for component variants and states
- Prefer `interface` over `type` for object shapes
- Use generic types for reusable components
- Always type function parameters and return values

### File Organization
```
app/
├── components/
│   ├── ui/           # Base UI components (Button, Card, Dialog, etc.)
│   ├── common/       # Shared business components
│   └── layout/       # Layout components
├── routes/           # Page components following file-based routing
├── hooks/            # Custom React hooks
├── store/            # State management
├── services/         # API and hardware service integrations
├── data/             # Static data and method configurations
├── utils/            # Utility functions
└── types/            # TypeScript type definitions
```

### Naming Conventions
- **Components:** PascalCase (e.g., `MethodExecutor`, `DeviceInteractionArea`)
- **Files:** kebab-case for routes, PascalCase for components
- **Variables/Functions:** camelCase
- **Constants:** UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- **CSS Classes:** Tailwind utility classes, kebab-case for custom classes
- **Interfaces:** PascalCase with `I` prefix for complex interfaces (e.g., `IMethodConfig`)

## UI Component Library

### Design System
- **Color Scheme:** Modern neutral palette with accent colors
- **Typography:** Clean, readable fonts with consistent sizing
- **Spacing:** Consistent spacing scale using Tailwind's spacing system
- **Borders:** Rounded corners (rounded-xl, rounded-2xl) for modern look
- **Shadows:** Elegant shadow system with hover effects
- **Animations:** Subtle transitions and hover effects (duration-200, duration-300)

### Component Patterns

#### Button Component
```tsx
// Use the custom Button component with consistent variants
<Button variant="default" size="lg" className="...">
  Action Text
</Button>

// Available variants: default, destructive, warning, outline, secondary, ghost, link, success, elegant
// Available sizes: sm, default, lg, icon, xl
```

#### Card Component
```tsx
// Use Card components for content grouping
<Card className="bg-card border border-border/50">
  <CardHeader>
    <CardTitle>Title</CardTitle>
  </CardHeader>
  <CardContent>
    Content here
  </CardContent>
</Card>
```

#### Layout Patterns
- Use `PageLayout` for consistent page structure
- Use `fixedHeight={true}` for full-screen layouts
- Follow the grid system: `grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-4`
- Use flexbox for component internal layouts

### Responsive Design
- Mobile-first approach with Tailwind breakpoints
- Use `lg:` prefix for desktop-specific styles
- Ensure components work on all screen sizes
- Test layouts at different viewport sizes

## Hardware SDK Integration

### Device Connection Patterns
```tsx
// Always check device connection before operations
const { currentDevice, sdkInitState } = useDeviceStore();

if (!currentDevice) {
  return <DeviceNotConnectedState showFullPage={true} />;
}

if (!sdkInitState.isInitialized) {
  // Show loading or initialization state
}
```

### Method Execution Pattern
```tsx
// Use MethodExecutor for consistent method execution
<MethodExecutor
  methodConfig={selectedMethod}
  executionHandler={handleMethodExecution}
  className="h-full"
/>
```

### Error Handling
- Use toast notifications for user feedback
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Log errors for debugging
- Provide meaningful error messages to users

## Routing & Navigation

### Route Structure
- Use file-based routing pattern
- Keep route components focused and delegate to business components
- Use consistent breadcrumb navigation
- Implement proper loading and error states

### Page Layout Consistency
```tsx
// Standard page structure
<PageLayout fixedHeight={true}>
  <div className="h-full flex flex-col">
    <div className="flex-1 flex flex-col px-4 py-2 min-h-0">
      {/* Breadcrumb navigation - fixed height */}
      <div className="flex-shrink-0 mb-3">
        <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbItems} />
      </div>
      
      {/* Main content - fills remaining space */}
      <div className="flex-1 min-h-0">
        {/* Content here */}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
```

## Specific Component Requirements

### Firmware Update Page
- **Remove device information display** - Don't show current device info cards
- **Remove back navigation buttons** - Maintain consistency with other method execution pages
- **Add file selection capability** - Implement file picker for firmware update methods
- **Use MethodExecutor pattern** - Follow the same execution pattern as other methods

### File Selection Implementation
```tsx
// For methods requiring file uploads, implement file selection
const handleFileSelect = async () => {
  const fileInput = document.createElement('input');
  fileInput.type = 'file';
  fileInput.accept = '.bin,.zip'; // Appropriate file types
  fileInput.onchange = (event) => {
    const file = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).files?.[0];
    if (file) {
      // Update method parameters with selected file
      setMethodParameters({ ...methodParameters, firmwareFile: file });
    }
  };
  fileInput.click();
};
```

### Method Configuration
- Define method configurations in `data/methods/` directory
- Use consistent structure for method presets
- Include proper descriptions and parameter documentation
- Support file upload parameters where needed

## State Management

### Store Patterns
```tsx
// Use custom hooks for state management
const { currentDevice, sdkInitState } = useDeviceStore();
const { methodParameters, setMethodParameters } = useHardwareStore();
```

### Context Providers
- Wrap app with necessary providers (I18nProvider, SDKProvider)
- Keep context focused and avoid over-nesting
- Use TypeScript for context value types

## Internationalization

### i18n Patterns
```tsx
// Use translation hooks consistently
const { t } = useTranslation();

// Use translation keys for all user-facing text
<Button>{t('common.execute')}</Button>
```

## Performance & Best Practices

### React Performance
- Use `useMemo` and `useCallback` for expensive computations
- Implement proper dependency arrays for hooks
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders
- Use React.memo for pure components when needed

### Code Quality
- Follow ESLint and Prettier configurations
- Write meaningful commit messages
- Add JSDoc comments for complex functions
- Keep components focused and single-responsibility
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks

### Testing Considerations
- Write components to be testable
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Add appropriate ARIA labels
- Test with different device states

## Security & Hardware Integration

### Hardware Security
- Never log sensitive data (private keys, seeds, etc.)
- Validate all user inputs
- Handle hardware disconnection gracefully
- Implement proper error handling for hardware operations

### Data Handling
- Sanitize user inputs
- Validate file uploads for firmware updates
- Handle large file uploads appropriately
- Implement proper loading states for long operations

## Development Workflow

### Code Organization
- Keep components small and focused
- Extract common logic into utilities
- Use consistent import ordering
- Group related functionality together

### Documentation
- Document complex business logic
- Add README files for major features
- Keep this .cursorrules file updated
- Document API integrations and hardware methods

## Common Patterns to Follow

1. **Consistent Error Handling:** Always use toast notifications for user feedback
2. **Loading States:** Show appropriate loading indicators during operations
3. **Device State Checks:** Always verify device connection before operations
4. **Responsive Design:** Ensure all components work on mobile and desktop
5. **Accessibility:** Use semantic HTML and proper ARIA labels
6. **Type Safety:** Leverage TypeScript for better development experience
7. **Component Reusability:** Create reusable components for common patterns
8. **Performance:** Optimize for smooth user experience

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

1. **Don't** hardcode strings - use i18n translations
2. **Don't** ignore TypeScript errors - fix them properly
3. **Don't** create overly complex components - break them down
4. **Don't** skip error handling - always handle potential failures
5. **Don't** use inline styles - use Tailwind classes
6. **Don't** ignore responsive design - test on different screen sizes
7. **Don't** log sensitive hardware data - respect user privacy
8. **Don't** create inconsistent navigation patterns - follow established routes
